hon's parser over the UTF-8 bytes: `YYYY-MM-DD`, `YYYYMMDD`, `YYYY-Www[-D]`, `YYYYWww[D]`, ASCII digits, the calendar and ISO week 53 checked, and — since the parser never looks for the end — the two bytes past an eight-byte date not read at all, so `20240101xx` passes). Facet patterns are `re.fullmatch` through `translatePyRegex(…, 'fullmatch')`, each translated once and run on `''` and `'Ā'` when `FacetPatterns` is built: a pattern outside the translator's subset, or one the host cannot run (`beyondHost`, shared with the criteria), makes it `unusable`, and testing such a pattern throws `PatternUnusable` — the one way out, never a guessed answer. `wireIssue(i, origin)` is the routes' `IssueOut` (`{severity, message, target_ids, category, check, origin}`), `issueOwner` the first target, `issueKey` the multiset key `(severity, category, check, message, target_ids)`. `DirtyCollector` is `core/validation/dirty.py`'s: an ordered set of ids (`ids` in first-insertion order) with the same hooks under camelCase names — `afterElementCreate`, `before`/`afterElementPropsChange`, `before`/`afterElementDelete` (over the applier's `containmentClosure`), `before`/`afterConnect`, `before`/`afterDisconnect`, `afterRelationshipPropsChange`, `addUniquenessGroupOf` — each adding exactly what the core's adds, every index set sorted by code point; a relationship type named in a uniqueness key (`out:` / `in:`, `keysOn`) re-keys the ends it names, so connecting, disconnecting or cascading it away also brings their old and new groups; fired from `applyBatch`, it collects the dirty set `_finalize` validates, held to `validation_dirty.json` in order. A rebase has no hook to fire, so `addNeighbourhood(model, ids, into)` over-approximates: per id, an element adds itself, its uniqueness group, its referencers, each outgoing relationship with its target and each incoming one with its source (each set sorted); a relationship itself, its ends and, for containment, its target's group; an id naming nothing itself — taken on the states before and after, it covers every entity whose verdict inputs moved. `IssueStore` is `ValidationState`: issues by owner, owners in insertion order; `replace(dirty, issues)` drops every dirty owner and files the new issues, an owner returning last, refuses an issue owned outside `dirty`, and says whether any owner's issues now differ (an owner that only moved does not count); `counts()` is kept as the store changes, severities in the order a walk of the store meets them. `storeListBody(store, rev, rulesStatus, tagOf?)` is `GET /model/issues`'s body over it — the first 5,000 issues in store order, `counts` exact, `truncated`, and the `rules_status` given, which `rulesStatusBody(compiled)` builds from a compile as the route does (`total`, `skipped` with `artifact_id, set_name, rule, reason`, `eval_errors`) — held with the store to `validation_steps.json`.
-  `LiveIssues(wc, {seed?, sweepStep?, sweepSkip?, rules?})` (`live.ts`) keeps an `IssueStore` over a working copy's WORKING state; nothing else may write to the working copy while it wraps it. It holds two compiles, `rules = {working, committed}` (W and C, both `EMPTY_RULES` unless given): W is the working layer's rule sets, the ones the store's issues are found with, C the committed layer's; with nothing staged they are the same. Its `stage`, `unstage` and `applyDelta` are the working copy's, each revalidating before it returns the ids whose verdict it may have moved, by three rules: a stage takes its applier's hooks, the server's own dirty set; a rebase (`unstage`, `applyDelta`) takes `addNeighbourhood` on the state before over every id it may touch — `touchedIds()`, plus every id a delta names or deletes — and on the state after over those and the ids its change set names, both passes carrying the ends of every relationship among them (from the model, the committed image of one staged away, or the delta's own `source_id` / `target_id`), since a relationship absent on one side cannot name its ends there and they move groups all the same; a merged edit (`coalesce`) takes its trial run's hooks and the rebase rule over the id it names and every batch it replays — from `mergePoint(op)` on, `touchedIds(at)` — since those batches may overwrite part of it or park; an edit that asks to merge and does not pays its hooks only. Every one of those dirty sets is then widened by W's reach (`expandScope`) on the state AFTER the transition — a stage after its hooks, as `_finalize` does, a rebase or a merged edit after its neighbourhoods — and revalidated with W: the premise of `src/rules/` holds because each dirty set holds every element whose properties or incident relationships moved, and the seeded invariants hold it with rules of two-hop atoms in play, the store equal to a fresh sweep after every action. The containment chain past its first link is the one input no rule reaches (the core shares it), which is why the seeded invariants compare stores without cycle issues. `version` moves when a splice changes the store's content (not when it only re-files an owner), on every applied delta (origins move with the rev), on every `setRules` (`rules_status` or the tags may have moved) and when the store becomes unusable. The sweep, `sweepSteps()`, is a `Steps<boolean>` whose state lives on the object, never in the generator: its first step takes the total, the entities then (`done: 0`), each later one walks on — elements in state order, then relationships — and validates and splices the next `sweepStep` entities (`SWEEP_STEP`, 512), a complete change of its own, so it resumes across any transition and any generator picks it up. It holds a live iterator per map, with the `orderEpoch` it was taken at and the greatest `ord` it has handed out: an entity deleted since is not reached (the transition that deleted it dropped it from the store) and one made since is reached, validated twice; when a re-sort moves the epoch it takes a fresh iterator and passes over every entity whose `ord` is no greater, at most `sweepSkip` (16 × `sweepStep`) a step — each was handed out already, or was put back at its old place by a transition that validated it. The progress counts the entities validated, short of the total until the last step, which alone reports `done === total` (entities made meanwhile can take the count past it); that step sets `seeded` and releases `whenSwept()`. A re-sort sends the walk back over what it passed, `sweepSkip` a step, so re-sorts more frequent than that would hold the sweep off its end. `restartSweep()` sweeps again from the start, in place: the store keeps what it holds meanwhile. `setRules({working, committed})` swaps both compiles, moves `rulesVersion` and `version` and drops the origins; it validates nothing. When W's ordered list of rule identities changes, `appliesPopulation(model, old W, new W)` joins the rescan queue after the ids still queued, each once — a rename or anything that leaves W's rules as they were queues nothing. The same generator drains the queue after any sweep due, `sweepStep` ids a step, each a `revalidate` with W as it is at that step, and yields `RESCAN_STEP` (a `SweepStep` marked `rescan: true`) in place of progress, so a rescan reports none and never moves `seeded`, which still means a sweep has ended. `settled` is false while the queue holds ids; `whenSettled()` resolves when it empties or the store becomes unusable, and `whenSwept()` waits for the rescan too. A `PatternUnusable` anywhere — `FacetPatterns` refusing a pattern at construction, or the host failing one mid-run, which is sticky — sets `unusable = 'pattern'`, empties the store, ends the sweep (`false`) and moves `version`; the transition itself still answers. `origins()` runs `probeStaged`, once per `(rev, stagedVersion, rulesVersion)` and not at all with nothing staged: `hooks` is the probe's dirty set (the server's `base_dirty`), `S` (`dirty`) is `hooks` widened by W's reach on the working state, and while W and C differ, every element a changed rule applies to — the rules one identity list holds more often than the other, both ways (ΔPop) — with its issues validated on the working state with W (`working`) and on the committed one with C (`committed`); `previewDirty` is `hooks` widened by C's reach and `preview` its issues on the working state with C, which is `working` itself when W and C hold the same rules in the same order. An element of ΔPop that neither W nor C reaches from `hooks` has the same inputs on both states, so its issues with W and with C are found on the working state alone and kept (`deltaOf`) for one rev and one pair of rule sets: a keystroke's probe does not pay for ΔPop again. With nothing staged in the model, `S` is ΔPop alone, empty when W = C. `tagScope()` is what the panel's tags read: every owner whose issues may differ between the two states, with its committed issues. The exact probe finds it (`S`, with `committed`); from there, while the store is seeded and settled, the rev and the rule sets hold and W's identities are C's, each transition adds the ids it dirties, with the store's issues from just before it revalidates them — no transition had dirtied them since the probe, so those were their committed issues (the dirty sets are exact, and each owner's issues are found independently of the rest of a scope) — and a keystroke's list runs no probe. A delta, a `setRules`, W ≠ C or a store not seeded or not settled drops it, and the next read probes; `resetTagScope()` drops it too. The three bodies (`bodies.ts`) answer as the routes do — `validateBody` and `previewBody` through `origins()`, whose `S` is part of their answer, `issueListBody` through `tagScope()` — so each throws `PatternUnusable` when the store is unusable: `issueListBody` is `GET /model/issues` over the working store, an issue whose owner the tag scope names `on_server` while a committed issue of that owner with the same `(severity, message, target_ids, category)` is left to match it and `uncommitted` past that, every other one `on_server`, and no `resolved` (the live list never shows a fixed issue), its `rules_status` W's — so a new rule's issues read `uncommitted` and a removed rule's are gone; `validateBody` is the staged branch of `POST /model/validate` — the store's issues of owners outside `S`, then `S`'s working issues tagged the same way, then the committed ones left unmatched as `resolved`, in their order; `previewBody(live, strict)` is the model half of `POST /commits/preview` over `preview`, all `on_server` — C's rules, as the server's preview never assigns a staged rule set — `would_block` being `strict` and a conformance issue the ops can be held to (`attributable_issues`): owned inside `hooks`, or any rule's, warnings included. Both staged bodies are held to `validation_steps.json`, whose second session carries rule sets.
