 - **`src/rules/`** — a port of `core/validation/rules/` (the rule grammar, compile, reach and the validator), line for line, held to `rules_compile.json`, `rules_eval.json` and `rules_reach.json`. The engine parses no YAML: the server parses each rule set once (`POST /rules/parse`, and `rules` on `GET /artifacts/payloads`) and hands on the definition as a JSON document in TEXT, which `readRuleSet` (`document.ts`) reads with the exact parser — bare `NaN` / `Infinity` as floats, integers past 2^53 as `bigint`, every float a `PyFloat` — and then strictly, against the grammar pydantic enforces: an unknown key, a wrong type, zero or two property tests (chosen by the key being there, so `equals: null` is a test), a `null` on a test other than `equals` / `not_equals`, an empty `all` / `any`, `count` without a bound, a relationship atom without exactly one non-null `exists` / `count`, more than 200 rules, a duplicate name or nesting past eight levels (a `where` one) all throw `RulesUnreadable`, the one way out, never a guess; where pydantic reads an explicit `null` as absence (`to`, `where`, `when`, `message`, a relationship's `exists` / `count`, a count bound) so does the reader, and absent defaults are filled. Each `Rule` keeps its `identity`, its entry as the document wrote it (`pyDumps` with `allowNan`). `compileRuleSets(sources, mm)` (`compile.ts`) compiles `RuleSource`s in the order given — `ruleSources(set, layer)` (`sources.ts`) lists them by name then id, both by code point: the committed layer's rule sets with their committed parse, or the working layer's, a staged payload's parse laid over, `'pending'` standing on the set's last parse and a `'pending'` create with none left out. A failed parse is one skip for the whole set (`rule` empty, `reason` the parse error's message); a disabled rule is dropped before the drift check; the drift check walks `when` then `then` and reports the first mismatch — an unknown `applies_to`, a property the DECLARED type lacks (a subtype's does not count), an unknown relationship type, an unknown `to`, a `where` checked against `to`'s properties and not at all without one — with `repr` (`pyRepr`), skipping the rule whole. A document the reader refuses, or a rule set that arrived without its parse, marks the compile `unreadable`: its rules are missing and it must not be used. A `CompiledRule` carries its `appliesTypes` (`elementDescendants(applies_to)`), `check` `rule:<name>` and reach `paths`; `rulesByType` lists them per exact type in compile order, and `identities` the compiled rules' identities. `appliesPopulation(model, …compiled)` is every element a rule applies to: types sorted, each type's ids sorted. `evaluate.ts` is `validator.py`: `pyRuleEq` is Python `==` with a `bool` equal only to a `bool` (numbers compare exactly across `int`, `bigint` and `PyFloat`, `NaN` never; `None` equals `None`, which a list item can be); a value is missing when absent, `null` or `[]`, and missing fails every test but `exists`; a list holds when any item does, `contains` on a list being membership and on a string a substring test (`pyContains`) with a string operand; the bounds need a number that is not a `bool`; a relationship atom counts the element's own adjacency of the type's descendants, the far element filtered by `to`'s descendants and `where`. An issue is `conformance` with the rule's severity and check, its message the rule's own when not empty, else `Rule '<name>' violated` plus `: <description>`, quotes literal. `RulesValidator` counts a rule that throws under its check and merges the run's counts into the compile's `evalErrors` once, in its global hook; nothing it runs can throw (every comparison is total, nesting is capped and a record cannot hold a dangling end), so `eval_errors` never moves on either side. `reach.ts`: `derivePaths` gives a root-first path of steps per relationship atom, a `where` extending its atom's path; `expandScope(model, compiled, dirtyIds)` walks, from the dirty ids that are current elements, every suffix of every path backwards (an `outgoing` step through `el.in`, an `incoming` one through `el.out`), keeps each element reached whose type the rule applies to, frontier sorted by code point, first-seen order — never filtering on the far types, so a retype on the path cannot hide an owner. Its premise: a rule's verdict on an owner reads the owner and its paths, so if a transition moves the verdict, some path of the owner, on the state before or after, first meets an element the transition dirtied (the stage hooks and the neighbourhood rule add both ends of every relationship they touch and every element they change), and the prefix up to it is on the after state too; walking back from the dirty set on the AFTER state therefore reaches the owner.
 - **`src/service/`** — `Scheduler` (`scheduler.ts`) runs engine work on the host's thread in slices. One pump, two lanes, each in arrival order: the _control_ lane (the replica's own transitions) runs in any state and first; the _model_ lane runs while the scheduler is open. A job is a `read` (one call), a `transition` (one call that changes the model, never interrupted — a transition may run up to 100 ms, AD-23) or a `scan` (a `Steps` generator that `run()` makes). A transition waits for every read that arrived before it and holds everything behind it; at each slice boundary of a running scan, the reads queued before the first transition are answered and other scans are skipped over, never started — so a scan never sees the model move under its iterators (probe: a `Map` iterator that crosses a `clear()` and refill reads everything again, and `Model.elements()` does exactly that on the first ordered read after a rewind). `cancel(id)` removes a queued job or drops a running scan at its next step, unanswered; closed (`setOpen(false)`), the model lane keeps its jobs and a scan that was mid-flight goes back to the head to start over, `run()` called again. The pump reads the injected clock after every unit — a read, a transition, one step — and at `SLICE_TARGET_MS` (8, half of CN-3's 16 ms chunk: at M a collector pause of 5–8 ms lands inside a step now and then) calls `onSliceEnd`, awaits `yieldToHost()` and starts a new slice — `onSliceEnd` also runs when the pump runs out of work, the end of that slice; messages are handled on those host turns only, never inside a unit. Idle and open, it gives its two background slots one step each in turn: the digest check (`setBackground`), whose `restartBackground()` drops its steps in flight (they published nothing) so a new run starts at its next idle step, and the sweep (`setSweep`), whose steps resume across any transition — `restartBackground` leaves it alone and closing only pauses it. A slot empties when its task ends. `pause()` lends the slice clock to work outside the pump (a snapshot open): `undefined` while the slice has room, else the host's turn. The pump starts on a microtask, so every job one message brings is queued before any runs.
